- Iterator subtraction within same container
WHAT: Subtracting two iterators or pointers that do not both refer to elements of the same container (or one-past-the-end of that same container) causes undefined behavior when computing distances or ranges.
WHY: Undefined behavior can lead to memory corruption, incorrect range checks, and exploitable crashes or data leaks.
HOW: Before subtracting iterators/pointers (including via std::distance), ensure both originate from the same container/array or its end(); avoid arithmetic or ordering comparisons on unrelated pointers/iterators.
EXAMPLE: // Noncompliant: test may not be in foo
// ptrdiff_t d = test - foo;
// Compliant: only subtract when known to be within same array
// if (test >= foo && test <= foo + n) { ptrdiff_t d = test - foo; }

- Do not use pointer arithmetic on polymorphic objects
WHAT: Avoid performing pointer arithmetic on pointers to polymorphic objects (objects with virtual functions), which can result in invalid object access and undefined behavior.
WHY: Miscomputed object addresses can corrupt memory, break type safety, and enable code execution or data leaks.
HOW: Use standard containers (such as std::vector) and iterators to traverse collections of polymorphic objects, and access elements via iterator dereference instead of manual pointer arithmetic.
EXAMPLE: Before (noncompliant, conceptual): `Base *p = arr; p = p + 1; p->virt();` After (compliant): `std::vector<Base*> v{new Derived}; for (auto it = v.cbegin(); it != v.cend(); ++it) (*it)->virt();`

- Guarantee that library and STL copy operations do not overflow destination containers
WHAT: Copying data into a container (array, std::vector, etc.) that is not large enough for the data (for example, using std::copy, std::memcpy, std::memmove, std::memset, std::fill, std::transform with an undersized destination) causes buffer overflow.
WHY: Buffer overflows can corrupt memory, cause crashes, and enable code execution or data leaks.
HOW: Before copying, ensure the destination container is sized to hold all elements plus any required terminators, or limit the number of elements/bytes copied to the destination’s current capacity/size; prefer APIs or patterns that grow the destination (e.g., insert iterators) instead of writing via raw/begin iterators.
EXAMPLE: Before (unsafe): std::vector<int> dest; std::copy(src.begin(), src.end(), dest.begin());
After (safe): std::vector<int> dest(src.size()); std::copy(src.begin(), src.end(), dest.begin());

- Guarantee sufficient storage for strings and null terminators
WHAT: Copying or reading strings into a buffer that is too small, or that does not reserve space for the null terminator, can cause buffer overflows (for example, using `std::cin >> buf` where `buf` is a fixed-size `char` array).
WHY: Buffer overflows can corrupt memory, cause crashes, and enable code execution vulnerabilities.
HOW: Always ensure the destination buffer is large enough for the maximum expected data plus the null terminator; when using input APIs, explicitly bound the number of characters read to `buffer_size - 1` and do not rely on stateful width settings that reset after use.
EXAMPLE: Before (unsafe, may overflow): `char buf[12]; std::cin >> buf;` After (safer): `char buf[12]; std::cin.width(sizeof(buf)); std::cin >> std::setw(sizeof(buf)) >> buf;`



